1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:05,182
Every organisation runs cryptography it cannot fully see. A call inside a payments service.

2
00:00:05,182 --> 00:00:09,814
A version pin in a dependency file. A cipher list in an nginx config nobody has opened in years.

3
00:00:09,814 --> 00:00:14,446
The signature algorithm on a certificate.

4
00:00:14,446 --> 00:00:19,078
A private key baked into a container image that shipped months ago.

5
00:00:19,078 --> 00:00:23,710
Six kinds of artefact — and no two describe cryptography the same way.

6
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:30,652
CryptoDrishti reads all of it, and turns it into one inventory you can defend.

7
00:00:33,660 --> 00:00:42,644
Seven sensors: source, dependencies, binaries, certificates, configuration, containers, and one live TLS endpoint the operator names.

8
00:00:42,644 --> 00:00:51,628
Every hit becomes one distinct cryptographic asset — and purpose and assurance are part of that asset's identity.

9
00:00:53,478 --> 00:00:57,957
A real scan of the demonstration estate. 23 distinct assets.

10
00:00:57,957 --> 00:01:02,437
16 of them do not survive a quantum computer.

11
00:01:02,437 --> 00:01:06,916
And the scan states its own completeness at the top, before a single finding.

12
00:01:10,078 --> 00:01:18,208
That one scan found five RSA findings.

13
00:01:18,208 --> 00:01:26,339
The first is a signing call — RSA-PSS padding — so the target is ML-DSA-65.

14
00:01:26,339 --> 00:01:34,469
The second is RSA-OAEP wrapping a key: that is key establishment, and the target is a hybrid key exchange.

15
00:01:34,469 --> 00:01:42,599
A completely different migration.

16
00:01:42,599 --> 00:01:50,730
The third is a cipher list that permits RSA without ever saying what for — so the tool names no target at all.

17
00:01:50,730 --> 00:01:58,860
Knowing the algorithm is not enough. Purpose decides the migration.

18
00:02:00,710 --> 00:02:06,526
Every finding carries the chain that produced it. The call site. The symbol matched.

19
00:02:06,526 --> 00:02:12,342
The technique — an AST analysis, not a keyword guess. The confidence.

20
00:02:12,342 --> 00:02:18,158
How strong the evidence is that the key is really used. What a quantum computer does to it.

21
00:02:18,158 --> 00:02:23,975
The resolved purpose. The exposure arithmetic. And only at the end, a target.

22
00:02:25,825 --> 00:02:35,064
That whole chain is in the product, on one screen, for every finding — including which inputs a human set by hand, and which are still defaults.

23
00:02:40,425 --> 00:02:50,609
It is just as careful about what it did not see.

24
00:02:50,609 --> 00:03:00,794
A container image is replayed layer by layer, so a key written in one layer and deleted in the next is reported as historical: gone at runtime, still extractable from the archive.

25
00:03:00,794 --> 00:03:10,979
And when the destination policy refused an endpoint, the scan is marked PARTIAL — and says which one, and why.

26
00:03:12,829 --> 00:03:18,749
The output is not a bespoke report.

27
00:03:18,749 --> 00:03:24,670
It is a CycloneDX cryptographic bill of materials — the published standard — carrying the asset type, the primitive, the resolved purpose and the evidence occurrence for every finding.

28
00:03:24,670 --> 00:03:30,590
Validated offline against the official JSON Schema, vendored at a pinned commit.

29
00:03:30,590 --> 00:03:36,510
CI fails the build on a violation.

30
00:03:39,429 --> 00:03:46,381
Scattered evidence becomes one explainable inventory, a risk assessment you can argue with, and a migration decision you can defend.

31
00:03:46,381 --> 00:03:53,334
CryptoDrishti · Team Zero-Day · SIH26164
